EI Paso Baseball Fans Turned Out  By PETE CICCARELLI
THE TARNISHED TROPHY sat on its once burnished mahogany base. One had to fight the long nets of cobwebs to
brush away the dust and in the gloomy light of Dudley Field’s ticket office read the inscription: EL PASO SUN KINGS —
GRAND SWEEPSTAKES WINNER — NATIONAL ATTENDANCE DERBY — 1961.
//
THE SUN KINGS were left in the desert with very little water to drink. There was no money in the till for advertising.
The 1970 Sun Kings contended for the Texas League Western Division title until the last series of the season. Attendance was
humiliating. As one player put it, “It’s hard playing every night with no one watching you, but I really can’t blame them, if I
weren't playing, I darn sure wouldn’t come out here.””
It wasn’t the sports fan that stripped El Paso of professional baseball, rather it was the game itself. Baseball stopped promot-
ing itself and in doing so made enemies out of long time supporters.
DUDLEY FIELD was 36 years old and had never been painted. The prices were high and the seats were uncomfortable.
The concessions area was dirty, the food poor and the prices ridiculous.
At season’s end Ray Johnston announced the withdrawal of his club from EI Paso claiming that there was no local support.
He was three months late, the long time El Paso baseball fans had surrendered m June.
